WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.252
A chemical identifier is plainly printed

00:00:03.272 --> 00:00:06.126
in a record, and a model can encode every

00:00:06.146 --> 00:00:08.844
piece of it. Searching still fails.

00:00:09.007 --> 00:00:11.229
Has that ruled out a representation

00:00:11.249 --> 00:00:13.703
problem? Pause and name the separate

00:00:13.723 --> 00:00:15.932
components between the string you see and

00:00:15.952 --> 00:00:18.406
the evidence a retriever can use.

00:00:20.831 --> 00:00:23.091
Out of vocabulary only means something

00:00:23.111 --> 00:00:25.604
relative to a named vocabulary.

00:00:25.779 --> 00:00:27.885
The index may lack a corresponding

00:00:27.905 --> 00:00:30.810
analysed term. A tokeniser may lack a

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.588
complete word entry.

00:00:32.763 --> 00:00:35.179
Or every token may exist while the

00:00:35.199 --> 00:00:38.207
retriever’s representation remains poor.

00:00:38.382 --> 00:00:39.985
Those are different conditions.

00:00:40.908 --> 00:00:43.418
Ask three questions separately.

00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:46.253
Is there a corresponding term and posting

00:00:46.273 --> 00:00:47.967
list in the lexical index?

00:00:48.129 --> 00:00:51.356
Can the model tokeniser encode the string?

00:00:51.505 --> 00:00:54.127
Has the retrieval model learnt a useful

00:00:54.147 --> 00:00:57.348
representation of those pieces in context?

00:00:57.906 --> 00:00:59.567
The first two concern literal

00:00:59.587 --> 00:01:01.926
vocabularies. The third concerns

00:01:01.946 --> 00:01:04.502
representation or training exposure.

00:01:04.678 --> 00:01:07.402
A tokeniser passing its question does not

00:01:07.422 --> 00:01:08.703
answer the other two.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:12.196
Inspect the field, lexical analysis, model

00:01:12.216 --> 00:01:14.773
tokenisation and retrieval behaviour as

00:01:14.793 --> 00:01:15.971
separate layers.

00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:19.031
An older word level model may have no

00:01:19.051 --> 00:01:21.299
complete entry for a new word.

00:01:21.461 --> 00:01:24.431
A subword model can instead encode known

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:27.691
pieces. That often avoids literal whole

00:01:27.711 --> 00:01:30.294
word absence, while making no promise

00:01:30.314 --> 00:01:32.060
about how informative the resulting

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.554
representation will be.

00:01:34.119 --> 00:01:35.927
The chapter’s appendix reports Bert

00:01:35.947 --> 00:01:38.298
splitting rizzlord into the three pieces

00:01:38.318 --> 00:01:40.991
shown here, rather than a neat rizz plus

00:01:41.011 --> 00:01:43.491
lord. That is a reported tokenisation

00:01:43.511 --> 00:01:46.185
example, not a tokeniser run for this

00:01:46.205 --> 00:01:49.355
film. Another model may split exactly the

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:51.004
same string differently.

00:01:51.931 --> 00:01:54.428
A pure byte level model can encode valid

00:01:54.448 --> 00:01:56.515
UTF eight input.

00:01:56.665 --> 00:01:58.604
A character level model depends on its

00:01:58.624 --> 00:02:01.580
supported character inventory or fallback.

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:04.093
Either can represent an expression’s form

00:02:04.113 --> 00:02:06.658
without having learnt how people use it

00:02:06.678 --> 00:02:08.178
for this retrieval task.

00:02:08.747 --> 00:02:11.923
So tokenisable does not mean understood,

00:02:12.162 --> 00:02:14.655
and understood in some language task does

00:02:14.675 --> 00:02:16.923
not establish good retrieval.

00:02:17.098 --> 00:02:19.565
The source’s new expressions illustrate

00:02:19.585 --> 00:02:22.090
why naming the representation matters.

00:02:22.253 --> 00:02:24.952
Avoid calling every weak response out of

00:02:24.972 --> 00:02:27.722
vocabulary when no literal vocabulary

00:02:27.742 --> 00:02:29.307
entry is missing.

00:02:30.225 --> 00:02:31.572
Now reverse the problem.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.614
The source supposes an identifier, delulu

00:02:34.634 --> 00:02:37.938
four twenty seven, occurs in three indexed

00:02:37.958 --> 00:02:40.825
documents. Our invented keyword field

00:02:40.845 --> 00:02:43.892
example stores that exact string in three

00:02:43.912 --> 00:02:46.508
records and a neighbouring identifier in a

00:02:46.528 --> 00:02:49.884
fourth. Literal equality selects the three

00:02:49.904 --> 00:02:51.430
intended identities.

00:02:51.988 --> 00:02:54.555
That calculation demonstrates exact field

00:02:54.575 --> 00:02:58.086
identity only. An ordinary text analyser

00:02:58.106 --> 00:03:00.650
might split the string into word and

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:02.028
numeric components.

00:03:02.203 --> 00:03:05.147
Query and document analysis must agree.

00:03:05.283 --> 00:03:08.382
BM twenty five can weight rare matching

00:03:08.402 --> 00:03:10.830
components without previously encountering

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:13.368
the identifier during model training.

00:03:14.273 --> 00:03:16.969
A standard single vector dense retriever

00:03:16.989 --> 00:03:19.946
usually can encode the identifier as model

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:23.554
tokens. Contextualisation and pooling may

00:03:23.574 --> 00:03:26.196
still blur it with related strings or

00:03:26.216 --> 00:03:29.198
concepts. A small local difference can

00:03:29.218 --> 00:03:31.608
determine relevance even when the overall

00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:33.102
meanings look similar.

00:03:33.642 --> 00:03:35.925
The chapter cites research showing that

00:03:35.945 --> 00:03:37.832
better training can mitigate this

00:03:37.852 --> 00:03:40.191
weakness. It does not claim dense

00:03:40.211 --> 00:03:43.334
retrieval always fails on identifiers.

00:03:43.497 --> 00:03:45.165
Our neighbouring strings are a teaching

00:03:45.185 --> 00:03:48.270
contrast, not measured vectors, model

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.164
scores or a reported ranking.

00:03:51.073 --> 00:03:53.527
Gene names, chemical strings, dataset

00:03:53.547 --> 00:03:56.929
titles, model names, article identifiers,

00:03:56.949 --> 00:04:00.048
personal names and new terminology can all

00:04:00.068 --> 00:04:02.006
make exact identity important.

00:04:02.169 --> 00:04:04.326
Preserve a suitable lexical route and

00:04:04.346 --> 00:04:06.942
inspect fields and analysis.

00:04:07.104 --> 00:04:09.507
Do not assume an embedding alone preserves

00:04:09.527 --> 00:04:10.434
the distinction.

00:04:10.988 --> 00:04:13.582
A synonym or controlled term may work

00:04:13.602 --> 00:04:15.979
around some lexical absence, while a

00:04:15.999 --> 00:04:18.466
keyword field may preserve an exact

00:04:18.486 --> 00:04:22.177
identifier. Neither remedy by itself fixes

00:04:22.197 --> 00:04:25.231
an unfamiliar deployment task or a failure

00:04:25.251 --> 00:04:26.545
to preserve negation.

00:04:26.708 --> 00:04:29.123
Choose the remedy for the component that

00:04:29.143 --> 00:04:30.579
lost the evidence.

00:04:31.477 --> 00:04:33.311
Use a known relevant record and a

00:04:33.331 --> 00:04:34.831
controlled contrast.

00:04:34.981 --> 00:04:38.066
Record the literal input, field selected,

00:04:38.099 --> 00:04:40.463
available analysis information and model

00:04:40.483 --> 00:04:42.228
tokenisation if documented.

00:04:42.403 --> 00:04:45.347
Establish eligibility first, then compare

00:04:45.367 --> 00:04:47.241
what each layer preserves.

00:04:47.378 --> 00:04:49.793
Mark internal details unknown when the

00:04:49.813 --> 00:04:52.086
system does not expose them.

00:04:52.661 --> 00:04:54.985
A good diagnosis can combine lexical

00:04:55.005 --> 00:04:57.523
absence, weak representation and

00:04:57.543 --> 00:04:59.597
unfamiliar deployment conditions.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.763
Tokenisation gives evidence about

00:05:01.783 --> 00:05:04.173
encoding, not a complete cause of

00:05:04.193 --> 00:05:05.461
retrieval failure.

00:05:05.623 --> 00:05:07.626
The next film considers failures where

00:05:07.646 --> 00:05:10.152
every word is familiar and the missing

00:05:10.172 --> 00:05:12.226
information is a relationship.
